      "question": "What is the CLIP model, and how does it learn joint image-text representations?",
      "category": "out_of_corpus",
      "subtype": "adjacent",
      "top1_score": 0.49260228872299194,
      "abstained": false,
      "response": "CLIP (Contrastive Language\u2013Image Pre-training) is a model that learns joint image-text representations by training on image-text pairs. It uses a contrastive loss to align image and text embeddings in a shared latent space, enabling zero-shot image-to-text conversion and cross-modal retrieval. As described in the survey, BLIP-2 leverages frozen image encoders alongside LLMs for efficient visual language pre-training, enabling zero-shot image-to-text conversions. The model architecture typically uses dual encoders (an image encoder and a text encoder) trained to maximize similarity between matching image-text pairs while minimizing similarity between non-matching pairs. This allows CLIP to understand and retrieve images based on text queries and vice versa, without needing task-specific supervision for each downstream task.",
